"Speed of Light: teaching notes",
"Speed of Light",
"Modern physics lab",
"{#} to {#} minutes (a full lab period with the table and fit)",
"Light takes a finite time to cross the bench: about {#} ns for a {#} m round trip. Foucault's method turns that time into an angle by letting a mirror rotate while the light is away.",
"A mirror turned by Δθ turns the reflected beam by {#}Δθ. That factor of {#} is the heart of the geometry.",
"The image shift at the microscope is Δs′ = 4AD²ω / (c(D + B)). It is tiny (about a quarter of a millimeter at {#} rev/s), so the measurement depends on reading the micrometer carefully, taking both rotation directions and fitting many speeds.",
"c follows from distances and a rotation rate you can measure on a bench, with no clocks fast enough to time {#} ns directly.",
"Students should know the law of reflection, angular speed ω = {#}πf, and the thin lens equation. Pick a setup and a seed to share if you want the class on one instrument. Turn on <strong>Blind</strong> before projecting the readings table, and turn off the digital micrometer reading if you want students to read the sleeve and thimble.",
"<strong>The bench at rest.</strong> Trace the beam: laser, L1 makes the point source S, the beam splitter, L2, the rotating mirror MR, out to MF and back. Ask why L2 must sit where it does (it focuses S onto MF). Center the crosshair on the spot and capture the rest reading.",
"<strong>Spinning at {#} rev/s.</strong> Ask Q1 first. The spot moves off the crosshair; follow it with the micrometer and read the shift, about {#} mm.",
"<strong>Slow motion: why the beam comes back crooked.</strong> One pulse goes out and back. The mirror's turn is drawn {#} times larger than it is; the readouts show the true Δt and Δθ. Point at the {#}Δθ return.",
"<strong>CW then CCW.</strong> The image moves to the other side of the rest reading. Ask why taking the difference is better than measuring from rest (Q4).",
"<strong>Longer path (D = {#} m).</strong> Predict the new shift before switching (Q3). Note that L1 has to move: A comes from the focus condition.",
"<strong>Measure c.</strong> A table filled by the procedure on this seed, labeled as simulated readings. Compare its fit with the class's own.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> Predict: with the mirror spinning at {#} rev/s, the light makes a round trip of 2D = {#} m. How many revolutions does the mirror make while the light is away? Is it enough to notice?",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> For D = {#} m, B = {#} m and f₂ = {#} mm, find A from the focus condition. Then find Δt, Δθ and the image shift Δs′ at {#} rev/s. Check with the demo.",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> If D is raised from {#} m to {#} m (B unchanged), by what factor does the shift grow? The light is away longer and the turned beam has a longer lever arm, which suggests ({#}/{#})² ≈ {#}. Why is the real factor much smaller?",
"<strong>Q4.</strong> Students measure the image at {#} rev/s clockwise and {#} rev/s counterclockwise instead of against the reading at rest. What is the difference between the two readings, and why is this the better measurement?",
"<strong>Q5.</strong> The micrometer has {#} mm divisions. How well must the shift be read to get c to {#} %? What does that tell you about why the lab fits many readings instead of using one?",
"<strong>Q6.</strong> A student forgets that a mirror turned by Δθ turns the reflected beam by {#}Δθ. What value of c do they get, and why?",
"<strong>Q7.</strong> D is measured with a tape to ±{#} cm at {#} m. Roughly what fractional error in c does that cause? Which is the larger worry, D or the image shift?",
"<strong>\"The image shift shows the light slowing down on the way back.\"</strong> The light travels at c both ways. The shift comes from the mirror having turned during the flight.",
"<strong>\"The beam comes back at the mirror's angle Δθ.\"</strong> Reflection doubles it: the returning beam is off by {#}Δθ.",
"<strong>\"Faster rotation makes the light take longer.\"</strong> The flight time 2D/c does not depend on the mirror. Faster rotation only turns the mirror further in that same time.",
"<strong>\"A longer path just makes the shift proportionally bigger.\"</strong> Δs′ grows roughly as D² / (D + B): more time for the mirror to turn, and a longer lever arm for the turned beam.",
"Δt = 2D/c, Δθ = ωΔt with ω = {#}π × (rev/s). The returning beam is turned by {#}Δθ, so the image of S on MF moves 2DΔθ, and L2 reimages it at the microscope with magnification A/(D + B): Δs′ = 4AD²ω / (c(D + B)), so c = 4AD²ω / ((D + B)Δs′).",
"The fit uses the slope k of reading against signed speed in rev/s: c = {#}πAD² / ((D + B)k). With CW and CCW readings, Δs′ = |s_CW − s_CCW| and ω = {#}π(f_CW + f_CCW).",
"A is set by {#}/f₂ = {#}/A + {#}/(D + B), with f₁ = {#} mm and f₂ = {#} mm.",
"Idealized: thin lenses, a perfect point source, MF exactly at the image of S, the mirror at constant speed. The real image is a train of short flashes as the turning mirror sweeps the beam across MF; it looks steady and is drawn steady.",
